  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
To understand how fading works, you need these 4 concepts:
  --> Material Color & Alpha Channel (R, G, B, A)
//--> A → Alpha (transparency)
1.0 (255) = fully visible
0.0 (0) = fully invisible
color.a = 0;   // invisible
color.a = 1;   // visible
2. Transparent Material Mode

Unity does NOT allow fading unless:
Material Rendering Mode = Transparent or Fade
Why?
Because opaque materials ignore alpha — they always render fully visible.
So before fading works:
✔ Material must support transparency
✔ Shader must use alpha blending

3. Coroutine = Code That Runs Over Time
Fading is a time-based animation.
Unity can’t run this in Update() without writing messy code.
So we use a Coroutine, which lets code run gradually:
waits each frame
updates something slowly
keeps Unity responsive
IEnumerator Fade()
{
    while (time < duration)
    {
        // fade logic
        yield return null; // wait for next frame
    }
}

4. Lerp (Linear Interpolation)
This is the magic part.
Mathf.Lerp(a, b, t) means:
👉 Start at a
👉 End at b
👉 t goes from 0 → 1 over time
float alpha = Mathf.Lerp(0f, 1f, t / duration);


